0,0,0,3,8,8,0,3,0,0,0,3,8,8,0,3,0],[0,0,8,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,8,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,8,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,8,3,0],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0],[0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0],[0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[0,8,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,8,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,8,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,8,0,3,0],[8,8,0,3,0,0,0,3,8,8,0,3,0,0,0,3,8,8,0,3,0,0,0,3,8,8,0,3,0],[0,0,8,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,8,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,8,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,8,3,0],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0],[0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0],[0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,3,0],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[0,8,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,8,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,8,0,3,0,0,0,3,0,8,0,3,0]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## What the grid is made of\n\nEvery input is a lattice. Full-length lines of one colour (2, 1, 8, or 3 in the test) run every 4th row and every 4th column, at positions 3, 7, 11, and so on. They cut the background into 3×3 cells. Exactly one cell holds a small coloured motif:\n- Pair 1: a 4-coloured diamond.\n- Pair 2: a 3-coloured blob.\n- Pair 3: a 2-coloured S shape.\n- Test: an 8-coloured diagonal shape.\n\n## How the output is built\n\nNumber the cells by block index: block row `R = y // 4` and block column `C = x // 4`. The motif's cell is at:\n- Pair 1: (2, 1). In the output, copies appear in block rows 0, 2, 4 and block columns 1, 3, 5. Those are exactly the cells with the same parity of R and C as the source.\n- Pair 2: (1, 1). Copies appear at R, C ∈ {1, 3, 5}, which again matches the parity.\n- Pair 3: (0, 5). Copies appear at R ∈ {0, 2, 4, 6} and C ∈ {1, 3, 5}. Block row 6 is the bottom strip y = 24, which is only one row high. There only the top row of the motif appears (`2 2 0`), so copies are clipped at the grid edge.\n\nThe rule is: **stamp the motif's 3×3 cell into every cell whose block row and block column have the same parity as the source cell**, clipping at the border. Nothing else changes, and the lines are never overwritten.\n\nThe spacing is read from the divider positions rather than hard-coded. The period is the gap between consecutive divider rows (or columns), and the cell origin is the cell size modulo the period.\n\n## The test input\n\n- Lines are colour 3, period 4.\n- The motif is in block (1, 4), at y 4–6 and x 16–18: rows `0 8 0`, `8 8 0`, `0 0 8`.\n- It gets copied to block rows 1, 3, 5, 7 and block columns 0, 2, 4, 6. Block row 7 starts at y = 28, which is the last grid row, so only the motif's top row `0 8 0` survives there.\n\n## Teaching stages\n\n1. Emit the stamping of each block row (one emit per row of copies).\n2. Emit the final canvas.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = background(g)\n    dv = dividers(g)\n    rows = sorted(y for y, c in dv[\"rows\"])\n    cols = sorted(x for x, c in dv[\"cols\"])\n    line_col = dv[\"rows\"][0][1] if dv[\"rows\"] else dv[\"cols\"][0][1]\n    py = rows[1] - rows[0] if len(rows) > 1 else 4\n    px = cols[1] - cols[0] if len(cols) > 1 else 4\n    sy, sx = py - 1, px - 1  # cell size\n    # cell origin offsets: cells start right after a divider\n    oy = (rows[0] + 1) % py\n    ox = (cols[0] + 1) % px\n    # locate motif\n    mask = (g != bg) & (g != line_col)\n    ys, xs = np.nonzero(mask)\n    R0 = (ys.min() - oy) // py\n    C0 = (xs.min() - ox) // px\n    y0 = oy + R0 * py\n    x0 = ox + C0 * px\n    motif = g[y0:y0 + sy, x0:x0 + sx].copy()\n    out = g.copy()\n    # all block row/col indices covering the grid (including partial ones)\n    Rmin = -((oy + py - 1) // py) if oy > 0 else 0\n    Cmin = -((ox + px - 1) // px) if ox > 0 else 0\n    R = Rmin\n    while oy + R * py < H:\n        if (R - R0) % 2 == 0:\n            yy = oy + R * py\n            stamped = False\n            C = Cmin\n            while ox + C * px < W:\n                if (C - C0) % 2 == 0:\n                    xx = ox + C * px\n                    for dy in range(sy):\n                        for dx in range(sx):\n                            y, x = yy + dy, xx + dx\n                            if 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W:\n                                out[y, x] = motif[dy, dx]\n                    stamped = True\n                C += 1\n            if stamped:\n                emit(out, f\"Stamp the motif into every same-parity cell of block row {R}.\")\n        R += 1\n    emit(out, \"All cells with the same block-row and block-column parity as the motif now carry a copy.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"panels":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The spacing is read from the divider positions rather than hard-coded."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The period is the gap between consecutive divider rows (or columns), and the cell origin is the cell size modulo the period."}],"repeat":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The period is the gap between consecutive divider rows (or columns), and the cell origin is the cell size modulo the period."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Lines are colour 3, period 4."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Exactly one cell holds a small coloured motif:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The motif's cell is at:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"There only the top row of the motif appears (`2 2 0`), so copies are clipped at the grid edge."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Those are exactly the cells with the same parity of R and C as the source."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Copies appear at R, C ∈ {1, 3, 5}, which again matches the parity."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"ule is: **stamp the motif's 3×3 cell into every cell whose block row and block column have the same parity as the source cell**, clipping at the border."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"1984974a7ecacd2826e913681dd2f15a8d02ba04a76b7a4f187f846490a4f86c","task_sha256":"737d7b8a60570d394ef389372a72c3a87ebf8726a7093cfc9e1716b0f7a5552c","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
